WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:09.720
PALAK DALAL BHATIA:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.950
Thanks for being here.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.510
I'm Palak.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.570
I'm a product manager on
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.460
and I have with me Ken.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.850
KEN OWENS: Hi, I'm Ken Owens.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.030
I'm a software engineer on
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.320
I work on the Workloads Team.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.347
And in open-source, I contribute
primarily to the workloads API.

00:00:25.347 --> 00:00:27.430
That's in the special
interest group applications,

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.464
and I'm a co-chair
of that group.

00:00:30.464 --> 00:00:31.880
PALAK DALAL BHATIA:
So today we'll

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.710
talk about how to build portable
data processing workloads

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.297
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:37.297 --> 00:00:39.380
The amount of data that
companies are dealing with

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.000
is exploding.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.250
Information is a
valuable commodity.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.840
There's an increasing
need to generate

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.120
relevant and intelligent
insights from that data

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.610
to make business
critical decisions.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.680
But doing so is not
easy, especially when

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.260
you're dealing with a humongous
amount of data and tools

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.800
and technologies that are
changing at a rapid pace.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.650
Users need a way to incorporate
intelligent decision-making

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.050
in their applications,
while iterating quickly

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.980
on their applications.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.430
They also want to use the
latest machine learning and data

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:11.090
processing technologies in a
way which is easy to understand,

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.420
and doesn't have a very
steep learning curve.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.390
Developers need to offload the
operational burden of actually

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.820
deploying and managing an
application in production

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.760
clusters, while at
the same time they

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.890
are asking for high
performance and acceleration

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.440
to get intelligent insights
at a lightning pace.

00:01:30.527 --> 00:01:34.280
Enter containers and Kubernetes.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.900
Containers are sealed
deployment units

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.450
that provide efficient
isolation, reusability,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.880
and portability.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.160
Through containers you can
modernize your infrastructure,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.190
and launch and iterate
on features much faster.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.300
Kubernetes has emerged as the
leading container orchestration

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.710
platform.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.390
It does all the
heavy lifting for you

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.910
for managing, operating,
upgrading, and scaling

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.130
your production clusters.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.840
Moreover, it is
open-source, so you

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.600
can move your applications
and workloads to any cloud

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.190
without any vendor login.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.460
Today, we will talk about
how Kubernetes provides

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.100
the best platform for
running those data processing

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.390
applications.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.260
We will show you a demo,
and a technical deep dive

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.090
on both Spark and Airflow
applications on Kubernetes.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.312
Over to you, Ken.

00:02:22.312 --> 00:02:24.270
KEN OWENS: So today I
want to talk a little bit

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.690
about the pieces that you use
to build a data processing

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.790
application on
top of Kubernetes.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.070
How many of you guys
are familiar with pods?

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:33.570
Most of you.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.111
So, most of you have
used Kubernetes,

00:02:35.111 --> 00:02:36.870
or have some experience with it.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.890
So, this might be a bit
of an overview for you.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.530
So Kubernetes is
all about resources.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.280
When you create a pod,
a volume, whatever,

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.020
it's mirrored by
some real object,

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.492
or some object in storage
inside of the system.

00:02:51.492 --> 00:02:52.700
At the base you've got a pod.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.201
This is your atom of
scheduling, resource allocation.

00:02:55.201 --> 00:02:57.200
It's basically a loosely,
or tightly coupled set

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.855
of containers, with a shared
name space, shared networking,

00:02:59.855 --> 00:03:00.565
and volumes.

00:03:00.565 --> 00:03:01.940
And they have a
shared lifecycle.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.510
They live together,
they die together.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.470
Usually you only run
one container per pod,

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.860
unless you've got side
cars, but sometimes you

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.090
run more than one.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.600
So volumes, PVs, and PVCs.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.550
These are the atoms
of durable storage.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.250
So a volume is basically
something that appears inside

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.600
of your pod on the file system.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.660
You can use it to
access your data.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.180
If it's not backed by a
PV, then it's ephemeral.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:27.680
It disappears and the pod dies.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.800
If it is backed by a
PV, on Google Cloud

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.340
it's backed by a
persistent disk.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:33.870
When the pod dies, your
data's still in the disk.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.170
PVCs are the way you
associate those PVs.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.801
It's the way you
provision those PVs.

00:03:37.801 --> 00:03:39.800
It's the way you get your
volumes into your pod,

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.679
and get your data off.

00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:43.220
That's interesting,
but you also need

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.550
to get data across the
network into a pod.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.500
To do that, you use services.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.375
And this is basically
the atom of networking.

00:03:50.375 --> 00:03:53.672
It programs network to
route traffic to pods.

00:03:53.672 --> 00:03:55.130
And it may provision
load balancers

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.380
if you expose it externally.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.060
So pods, it's the basic
workload you can run.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.870
Just one single pod, right?

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.150
But it's not very interesting.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:06.390
In comes the workloads API.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:10.680
So deployments
and replica sets--

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.440
these are for your
12 factor apps.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:13.970
It might not be
interesting for data

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.310
processing in specific, like
your core data processing

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.240
workloads.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:18.740
But you still need
to 12 factor apps

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.573
in order to access those
workloads, in order

00:04:20.573 --> 00:04:22.400
to have administrative consoles.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.380
And all a deployment
is, is basically,

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:29.310
I'm going to replicate this pod,
in an identical way n times.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.700
And when I update it, I update
it in a consistent manner,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:32.908
but I can do it concurrently.

00:04:32.908 --> 00:04:37.260
So if I have three pods, I
can update two at a time.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.780
Not particularly
good for databases.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.738
Not particularly good
for stateful applications

00:04:40.738 --> 00:04:42.350
in general.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.060
That is why we
created StatefulSets.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.812
So StatefulSet creates n pods in
the same way a deployment does.

00:04:47.812 --> 00:04:49.520
But these pods are
different in that they

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.820
have durable provision
storage, and they also

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.430
have a durable network identity.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.470
And what that means
is if you kill the pod

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.750
and you bring it back,
it gets the same storage.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:03.172
It gets the same C name, it
gets the same SRV records.

00:05:03.172 --> 00:05:04.880
The rollouts are
slightly different, too.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:05.660
They're not concurrent.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.070
They're ordered, and sequential.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:08.704
Meaning that if I
have three pods,

00:05:08.704 --> 00:05:10.370
I wait till pod one
is running and ready

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.262
before I launch pod two.

00:05:12.262 --> 00:05:15.350
If pod two dies, I
don't kill pod one

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.920
until pod two is
completely terminated.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.330
And then DaemonSets
create one pod

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:22.580
for every node in the cluster.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:24.380
From a perspective
of data processing,

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:27.005
if you're running something like
HDFS, or any other distributed

00:05:27.005 --> 00:05:28.430
file system, your
data nodes, you

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:31.230
want to put them on a particular
set of nodes in the cluster.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.680
You don't want n of them
when a new node comes up.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:35.320
You want a new data node.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:39.160
DaemonSets allow you to do that.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.670
So those are all interesting
from the perspective

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.690
of workloads that
run continuously.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:47.190
The pods of a StatefulSet,
if they do terminate they get

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:47.700
restarted.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.059
Same for deployment,
same for a DaemonSet.

00:05:50.059 --> 00:05:52.350
But with Batch Workloads,
you have a difference, right?

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.580
They run to completion.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.280
If I start a Spark job, I want
the job to eventually stop

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:58.050
running when it's completed.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.710
Maybe I want it to
restart if it failed.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.370
That's where job comes in.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.290
Creates pods that
run to completion,

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.240
counts numbers of successes.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.790
And once a specified number
of successes have occurred,

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:12.090
the job terminates, and it says
it's completed successfully.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.780
And CronJobs are just a way
to manage time-based jobs.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.316
You can do one offs, you
can do repeating ones that

00:06:18.316 --> 00:06:19.440
run at a specific interval.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.045
So, the one thing
we realized when

00:06:24.045 --> 00:06:25.575
we were building
the Kubernetes API,

00:06:25.575 --> 00:06:28.350
is that we can build
you primitives,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.050
but we can't build
every primitive

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.340
you're ever going to need.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.066
So none of the resources
are actually special.

00:06:35.066 --> 00:06:36.440
You can bring your
own resources.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.210
These are custom
resource definitions.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:42.300
And it basically allows you just
to define your own resource.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.750
If you define job
as the resource

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.000
that we use for the
batch view on job,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.260
you can create a Spark job.

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:52.110
You can create an
Airflow cluster.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:57.060
And these become part of the API
in fundamentally the same way

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.550
as any other resource.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.140
To act on them, though, you need
to bring a custom controller.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.560
So all the resources that
we've talked about so far--

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.114
each have a controller
that ships with Kubernetes.

00:07:07.114 --> 00:07:09.030
But you can easily install
your own controller

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.890
to act on the
resources you define.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:15.570
So, we've talked a lot about
the pieces of these data

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.850
processing pipelines.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.090
The question is, how do I
get those on to my cluster?

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.160
So one way to do this
is using manifests.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.170
And a manifest is
basically just a YML file

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:26.670
that contains most
of those atoms

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.230
that we talked about before.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.990
You deploy it, you
apply it, it runs,

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.900
and it creates the
workload for you.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:35.912
The problem with
this is, if you're

00:07:35.912 --> 00:07:37.620
trying to get something
off of the shelf,

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.800
like go get a YML file that
somebody else has created

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.770
and deploy it into your
cluster, what if your monitoring

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:47.820
solution isn't the same as
the one that was found online?

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:51.390
Or what if your
lifecycle management

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:53.980
solution is totally different?

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:57.760
The manifest isn't really
portable from that perspective.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.190
So in comes Helm.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.770
Now Helm is a Kubernetes
package manager,

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.500
and what it allows you to do
is to template those manifests

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.510
and apply a set
of values to them.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:09.390
So, it still doesn't solve
for lifecycle management.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.480
But let's say you have
a template for the login

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.320
solution.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.050
You can just plug that
in via values.yml.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:16.680
If you want to
change configuration,

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.740
a values.yml will allow
you to change that.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.610
But really for
complicated orchestration,

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:25.060
which is typically the use case
we see for data processing,

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.610
it's still not sufficient.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:31.350
So, if you combine a custom
controller with a custom

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.120
resource, you get
what's colloquially

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.690
called the operator pattern.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.419
So the operator
pattern is basically,

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:39.720
I'm going to teach my cluster to
understand what a Spark job is.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.220
Then I'm going to
write a controller

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:43.049
to act on that
Spark job, and I'm

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:45.690
going to encode the day one and
day two operations necessary

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.270
for keeping that cluster up
inside of that controller,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.720
inside of that operator.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.720
And that seems to be the
dominant way that we're

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.160
approaching the problem.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:56.040
My colleagues are going to
give you some demos later on,

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.050
contributions that we've
made to the community,

00:08:58.050 --> 00:08:59.010
in particular for this.

00:09:02.212 --> 00:09:03.670
PALAK DALAL BHATIA:
So, just diving

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.710
a bit deeper into operators.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:08.470
For users to really trust
Kubernetes to run production

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:10.450
workloads like
databases, it needs

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:12.100
to be super easy to automate it.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.620
And the way to scale it
is to actually automate

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:15.880
how you manage it.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.490
An application
operator is basically

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:20.080
like a robot,
which performs some

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:23.320
of those functions of an
app's [? ops group. ?]

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:24.190
Think of a DBA.

00:09:24.190 --> 00:09:27.260
How critical a DBA
is to the enterprise.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:29.890
So this is really useful
for stateful applications.

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.020
They encapsulate the
operational concerns

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:34.600
of running an application
on Kubernetes,

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.320
and they run these applications
in a declarative fashion,

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.370
which Kubernetes understands.

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:42.010
The control loop observes
the existing state.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:44.140
It looks at what the
desired state is.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.780
It sees what difference is
needed, and then acts upon

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:50.500
the system to actually
reach that desired state.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:57.080
Now I'll talk about how Google
Kubernetes Engine is providing

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:58.760
some of the best
tools and technologies

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.420
to run those data
processing workloads.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:04.640
We recently launched
a marketplace

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:06.830
called GCP Marketplace,
and recently launched

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:08.940
the Kubernetes
section within it.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.600
So what that means is now
the GCP Marketplace has

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:13.580
native integration into GKE.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:17.270
You can click to deploy
stateful applications on GKE,

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:22.010
view those applications on the
UI as well as on your console.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.770
We made available first-party
Google Developed solutions

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.870
like Spark and
Airflow to start with,

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:31.670
as well as several third-party
solutions by vendors.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:34.670
Besides that, we have also been
leading open-source efforts

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:37.100
to develop something
called an app resource.

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.230
Previously, there was no
concept of an application

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:40.349
in Kubernetes.

00:10:40.349 --> 00:10:42.140
There were a bunch of
workload controllers,

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:45.320
like DeploymentSets,
StatefulSets, and others,

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:47.330
but we didn't define
an application.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:49.190
With this app
resource, you can now

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:54.650
view and manage an application
effectively on Kubernetes.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.650
You can also leverage some of
the cutting edge data services

00:10:57.650 --> 00:10:59.420
that GCP provides.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:04.570
For instance, BigQuery, CloudAI,
Stackdriver, TPUs, and GPUs.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:07.550
You can natively integrate those
on your Kubernetes applications

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:08.900
on Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:11:12.866 --> 00:11:14.490
So, the first operator
that we launched

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:15.990
was the Spark Operator.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:17.850
We had seen from users
that many of them

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.150
want to run data processing
and batch analytics

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.110
workloads on Kubernetes,
in a way, which

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:23.250
is native to Kubernetes.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:28.010
They wanted to use kubectl
when running their Spark jobs.

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.330
The Spark Operator is
available on GitHub as well as

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.280
on the GCP Marketplace.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.680
And I would like to invite
Yinan Li, who leads the Spark

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.910
Operator, to give a demo and
talk more about the operator.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:41.231
YINAN LI: Thanks, Palak.

00:11:48.475 --> 00:11:51.100
So, before I start, I just want
to get a sense, how many of you

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.090
run a Spark on
Kubernetes already?

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:54.780
OK, quite a few.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.810
OK, hopefully the number will
get bigger after the talk.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:00.530
So, the Spark
Operator is basically

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:04.260
a project that follows the
recent operator pattern.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:08.550
So, at its core, there's
some custom resource types--

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.720
and also the corresponding
custom controllers

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.190
that extend the
Kubernetes API and control

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.740
plane with application domain
knowledge-- in this case,

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:17.730
will be Spark.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:21.670
So there's actually two
types of custom resources.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:25.990
One is called Spark application,
which is one time Spark jobs.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.470
There's also a scheduled
Spark application time, which

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:30.580
is for time-based applications.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:33.710
So you can actually
run a cross schedule

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:37.560
for your Spark applications.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.855
The Spark Operator is basically
a custom controller that

00:12:41.855 --> 00:12:43.940
watches these custom resources.

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:46.260
And once it sees
them, if there's

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:48.180
any new resources
coming, or there's

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:50.430
any updates to
existing resources,

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:52.170
it will actually take actions.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:55.560
So in the case of
Spark, it will actually

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.250
run spark-submit for you.

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.610
So you don't need to
run that manually.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.530
And once the application
gets submitted,

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:06.870
it will actually monitor to
the pods for the applications

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:11.000
for you, and aggregates
status into the status section

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.960
of your custom resource object.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.660
So you can actually
get the status

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.580
of your application
in one place instead

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:23.190
of actually going through
all the pods individually.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:27.720
So let me switch to
the demo machine.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:34.410
So, this is where
the project's hosted.

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.590
Feel free to take a
look and try it out.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:41.880
So before I start,
the first thing

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:45.530
I'm going to show
you actually, is

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:47.260
how an application looks like.

00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:56.740
So this is a Spark
Application custom resource.

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.480
So you can actually
see, it allows

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:01.800
you to specify pretty much
every piece of information

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:04.500
your Spark application needs.

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:06.150
What is the main
class you want to use?

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.740
What is the main application
file you want to use?

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.790
In this case, the
application-jar

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.800
is actually hosted on GCS.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.040
And runtime will be
downloaded by the driver

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.250
before it gets launched.

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:23.095
And you can also specify
the application-arguments

00:14:23.095 --> 00:14:24.720
and what kind of
image you want to use,

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:28.980
and Spark Hadoop
configuration you want

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.910
to use for your application.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:33.210
And then it has
a section for you

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:36.300
to specify all
your dependencies.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:39.540
You can actually use remote
dependency on HDV servers,

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:44.760
or GCS, or on your
on-prem HDFS clusters.

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:47.550
And there's also
separate sections for you

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:51.600
to specify how the driver and
executor should look like.

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:59.350
In this particular case, the
application I'm going to demo

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:03.958
is one that tried to
find out on GitHub,

00:15:03.958 --> 00:15:06.640
what are the goal
on GitHub projects

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:09.820
that needs help from
contributors the most?

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:12.290
So this particular
application will actually

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:16.880
read public GitHub
data set on BigQuery.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.480
And then writes the
result back to BigQuery.

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:24.110
And intermediate result
will be written to GCS.

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.912
It's actually interacting
with both BigQuery and GCS

00:15:26.912 --> 00:15:27.620
at the same time.

00:15:30.330 --> 00:15:32.730
OK, so, the first
thing I'm going to do

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:36.150
is to create this
custom resource object.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:41.010
For this, you can either
use kubectl or the Spark

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:42.900
Operator also ships
with a command

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.970
line tool called
Spark Control, which

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:48.212
makes dealing with these
custom resources even easier.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:59.440
So, what happens, this actually
creates this custom resource

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:00.840
to the API server.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:06.160
So once that's done,
the custom controller

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.210
will actually see this new
custom resource object,

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:12.400
and will actually run
spark-submit for you,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.900
and that will create
the driver pod.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.590
So after that, I'm going to
demo how you can actually

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:19.060
grab logs from the driver.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:27.300
So you can see this is actually
grabbing the driver logs.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:29.250
You can also do streaming.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:33.820
This will basically be there,
and when more logs come in,

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:36.960
it will show you.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.350
You can also actually not
only grab the driver log,

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:43.200
also logs of executor pods.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.690
So to do that, you just
use the dash-E option,

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:49.320
then you basically tell
it what is that executor

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.970
ID you're interested in.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.580
This case, if I say,
one, so this actually

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:56.910
fetched the logs
off executor one.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:00.270
So that's for logs.

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:03.400
So, this command
line tool also comes

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:09.270
with the status
sub-command for you

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:12.810
to actually check what is the
current status of the app.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:16.680
Or show you the driver pod name,
where the driver is running.

00:17:16.680 --> 00:17:21.240
And also the individual states
of each individual executor

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:21.990
pod.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:27.970
So you can also use kubectl
describe to do the same thing.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:32.910
It will give you also the entire
spec of your custom object,

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.490
as well.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:37.680
So the next thing
I'm going to show

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:41.610
is actually to see how we can
access this Spark driver UI.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.400
So the UI is running
inside the driver.

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:50.040
To do that, I'm going
to do port forwarding

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:55.080
from my local machine
to the driver pod.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.390
So if you actually
use kubectl, you

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:01.205
have to basically know what
is the name of the driver pod.

00:18:01.205 --> 00:18:03.330
But if you use sparkctl,
you don't need to do that,

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:07.110
because we know where
the driver is running.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.207
So we can do that automatically.

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:19.110
OK, so, this is actually
forwarding from 4040 to 4040

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:20.450
on the driver pod.

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:31.590
So you see the driver
UI, and it gives you

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:34.480
information about
individual executor as well.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:40.080
So in this case, the actual
stages haven't started.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:43.260
But you get a sense how
you can access the driver

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:45.270
UI using port forwarding.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:47.800
So, this is actually
pretty straightforward.

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:54.490
So, the app is actually
running on a GKE cluster here.

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:58.950
So, if I do a
refresh, you can see

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.630
one driver pod got launched.

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:07.255
Then it's actually created
three executor pods, So off

00:19:07.255 --> 00:19:10.050
and running.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:15.360
So, let's stop this.

00:19:18.506 --> 00:19:22.590
Once your app is done, you
want to get rid of these pods.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:27.000
What you can do is
actually just to do this.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:31.680
Then it will delete your Spark
application custom object,

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:36.540
and in turn, you know all your
pods will be gone after this.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.510
So, this is pretty much
what I want to demo today.

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:41.640
But you can definitely
check out the documentation

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:46.380
on the GitHub site about other
features that we provide.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.419
You can also run
Chrome-based applications.

00:19:49.419 --> 00:19:51.960
And also, you can even actually
use the sparkctl command line

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:56.320
tool to automatically shape
your local dependency to GCS.

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.300
So you don't have
to do that manually.

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:00.900
So what happens if you
have local dependencies

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:04.050
in your application
specification,

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.630
sparkctl will actually
see them, and upload them

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:09.040
to a bucket you specify.

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:13.410
Then will actually
do a substitution

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:18.060
from these local dependencies to
the corresponding ones on GCS.

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:21.690
Then submit the converted
Spark application spec

00:20:21.690 --> 00:20:23.550
to the API server.

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.490
So you don't need to do these
kind of things manually.

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:28.440
Everything is handled for you.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:33.180
So, definitely go ahead and
check out our GitHub site

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.890
about documentations,
those kind of things.

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:38.400
So we've also got Slack channel.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:42.540
Feel free to join, and
post questions or comments.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:43.500
Thanks.

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.810
Next I'm going to hand
it over to my colleague,

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:49.590
Barney, who works on Airflow
and elastic search, those kind

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:50.820
of things on Kubernetes.

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:52.890
So he will be giving
a demo on the Airflow

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:55.071
Operator on Kubernetes.

00:20:55.071 --> 00:20:55.570
Thanks.

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:03.730
BARNEY: Hi, my name is Barney.

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:08.620
I work in Google as part
of the Kubernetes team.

00:21:08.620 --> 00:21:11.860
I'm here to present about
the Airflow Operator.

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:16.130
Before I tell what the
Airflow operator does,

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:19.150
let's take a look at
what an Airflow is.

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:23.800
Airflow is a platform
for deploying, managing,

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.275
scheduling, and
monitoring workflows.

00:21:26.275 --> 00:21:30.220
A workflow is a DAG,
Directed Acyclic Graph,

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:31.230
written in Python.

00:21:35.432 --> 00:21:37.060
A typical installation
of Airflow

00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:41.260
has many components, like
the UI scheduler workers,

00:21:41.260 --> 00:21:43.660
and the broker and
the sequel node.

00:21:46.190 --> 00:21:48.940
So deploying Airflow
is a complex endeavor,

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:51.730
with many moving parts, and
different configurations

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:54.180
for each of the components.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:59.980
The way it's typically deployed
right now is using Helm charts.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:02.860
But like Ken mentioned,
that does not

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.530
take care of the lifecycle
management of an Airflow

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:07.620
deployment.

00:22:07.620 --> 00:22:10.690
And also the developer
experience with an Airflow

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:15.520
is lacking, because you need
to copy the DAGs that you

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:19.970
are developing into the cluster,
before you can test it out.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:25.210
And if you expect individual
developers to stand up

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:30.280
their own clusters, it's not
an easy thing for them to do.

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:34.540
And this where the
Airflow Operator comes in.

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:38.710
The Airflow Operator
is a custom resource.

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:42.520
It's a bunch of custom resources
with a custom controller.

00:22:42.520 --> 00:22:46.630
What it does is, it makes
it dead easy and simple

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:48.880
to deploy Airflow on Kubernetes.

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:56.170
I just mentioned
custom resources.

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:58.780
A custom resource is
a declarative spec

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:03.980
that tells you what to
do, and not how to do it.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:05.440
And there are two
custom resources

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:09.979
that the Airflow Operator works
on-- the base, and the cluster.

00:23:09.979 --> 00:23:11.770
Let's switch to the
demo and I can show you

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:13.875
how the resources look.

00:23:13.875 --> 00:23:14.910
All right, there you go.

00:23:34.906 --> 00:23:36.780
I hope you guys can see
it all in the screen.

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:37.779
All right, there you go.

00:23:37.779 --> 00:23:39.270
This is the base resource.

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:42.390
The base resource has
two components, a MySQL

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:44.430
and NFS storage.

00:23:44.430 --> 00:23:47.280
The cluster resource has the
rest of Airflow components,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:53.110
like the UI scheduler
worker nodes and the broker.

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:57.720
So if you notice, the CRD spec
for the base is very minimal.

00:23:57.720 --> 00:23:59.730
You don't have to
specify the images,

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:03.810
or how you bring up
MySQL, how do you

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:07.600
set up the database inside it.

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:09.900
The username, the root
password, all of it

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.222
is taken care of by
the Airflow Operator.

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:19.690
But you could customize
the custom resource

00:24:19.690 --> 00:24:23.350
by specifying your own resource
requirements or placement

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:24.010
properties.

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:32.240
The good thing is, it just uses
kubectl to create and manage

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:33.930
these custom resources.

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:36.440
Let's deploy this.

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:41.346
And before that, let
me start the Operator.

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:55.419
So I'm creating the
custom resource.

00:24:55.419 --> 00:24:57.210
And you can notice on
the logs on the left,

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:02.640
that the Operator would start
picking up this custom resource

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:07.300
and deploying the StatefulSets
and other resources that

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:08.550
are needed for standing it up.

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:17.105
You see that it created the
MySQL StatefulSet as well

00:25:17.105 --> 00:25:18.880
as an NFS StatefulSet.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:23.379
And you also see it created a
secret dynamically for setting

00:25:23.379 --> 00:25:24.670
up the root password for MySQL.

00:25:29.128 --> 00:25:31.320
And the pod's already
at this point.

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:41.230
Let's look at the
other custom resource.

00:25:45.730 --> 00:25:49.370
So this is a cluster
custom resource.

00:25:49.370 --> 00:25:53.450
We can pick the executor
of choice for Airflow.

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:56.210
But what I'm using
is CeleryExecutor,

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:59.570
which is a multi-node
deployment of Airflow.

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:01.820
And we use Redis as the broker.

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:06.440
And all we need to say is which
version of the Airflow image

00:26:06.440 --> 00:26:09.500
that you want to use.

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:11.360
And you also notice
that there's the DAG

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:13.940
section, where you specify
where the DAGs need

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:15.360
to be pulled from.

00:26:15.360 --> 00:26:18.230
And this makes it dead
simple for someone

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:21.830
using Airflow to specify
all the their environment

00:26:21.830 --> 00:26:25.200
in the same file, and
let the Operator do

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:26.270
the deployment for them.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:40.710
I'm creating the cluster CRD.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:42.920
And you notice that the
operator is deploying

00:26:42.920 --> 00:26:47.760
the parts and the
StatefulSet Notice

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:50.240
that two new secrets
have been created--

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:53.210
one for Redis and one
for the scheduler.

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:57.470
So what the operator does is, it
creates a database dynamically

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:04.130
in MySQL for this instance
of Airflow cluster.

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:05.650
So you have the
UI, you have Redis,

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:07.400
you have a scheduler,
and two worker

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:10.101
nodes that are up and running.

00:27:10.101 --> 00:27:11.570
We'll give it a
couple of seconds,

00:27:11.570 --> 00:27:13.690
and try to access this
to see if it's up.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.860
Typically we do port
forwarding to access it,

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:20.180
because it's easier that way.

00:27:38.290 --> 00:27:40.230
So your Airflow cluster is up.

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:43.340
We can run a quick test to
make sure that it's working.

00:27:43.340 --> 00:27:44.145
Run a DAG.

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:56.490
So these are the
tasks it's running.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.000
Once it's completed, I will
show you how the DAG looks like.

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:06.120
It's almost done.

00:28:06.120 --> 00:28:08.180
There it goes.

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:12.110
So it shows that it ran
the DAG three times.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:15.440
This was triggered by me.

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:19.510
And if you open the Dag, this
is how the steps within the DAG

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:20.330
look like.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:24.620
So we have deployed a
working Airflow cluster

00:28:24.620 --> 00:28:29.150
within a couple of minutes,
and we were able to test it.

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:31.040
Let's take a look at
the configuration here.

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:37.885
You notice that it's set to be--

00:28:37.885 --> 00:28:40.430
the executor is CeleryExecutor.

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:46.720
And if you look at the
connection string for MySQL,

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:48.310
you notice that
it auto-generates

00:28:48.310 --> 00:28:50.960
a username, password, as
well as the database that

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:54.530
needs to be used for
this Airflow instance.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:57.260
And it connects
to the MC base SQL

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:59.560
that we deployed earlier
as part of the base CRD.

00:29:03.045 --> 00:29:06.600
So MC base, MySQL.

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:10.160
So I just demoed how easy it is
to deploy a multi-node Airflow

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:13.049
cluster using two CRDs.

00:29:13.049 --> 00:29:14.840
Let's go ahead and
deploy one more cluster.

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:25.640
All right, this time I'm going
to deploy Celery with the GCS

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:26.750
as the DAG source.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.680
Maybe I'll show how it
looks like before deploying.

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:40.340
You notice it's almost
similar to the earlier cluster

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:44.180
configuration, except that
you point to the GCS bucket

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:45.636
as DAG source.

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:16.080
All right, it's deployed
the second Airflow cluster.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:19.200
If you notice, I
didn't deploy the base

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:20.290
for the second cluster.

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:23.430
It reuses the base
from the first cluster.

00:30:23.430 --> 00:30:29.040
You could do it this way
to improve resource usage,

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:30.870
and this is particularly
useful if you

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:33.510
want disposable
development clusters

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:37.910
that you want to hand it
over to the developers.

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:40.260
Now, let's try connecting
to the new cluster.

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:10.930
And this is the second
cluster we just deployed.

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:14.260
And notice that it doesn't
have the state here,

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:17.608
because it's brand new.

00:31:17.608 --> 00:31:22.430
And if you look at the
configuration for My SQL,

00:31:22.430 --> 00:31:23.230
let's go back here.

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:30.460
If you notice it has a
different connection string,

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:32.260
because these were
dynamically generated

00:31:32.260 --> 00:31:34.290
for the second cluster.

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:37.480
And this is how we are
able to share the same base

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:39.985
across components.

00:31:39.985 --> 00:31:43.480
I'm sorry, clusters.

00:31:43.480 --> 00:31:48.110
All right, let me go ahead
and deploy one more cluster.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:49.750
And this is something new.

00:31:53.726 --> 00:31:55.217
OK, that's cluster.

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:04.840
There is something different
about this cluster.

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:09.810
If you notice, it doesn't
deploy the worker nodes.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:12.720
It just deploys the
UI and the scheduler.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:19.140
The reason is, this cluster uses
the Kubernetes executor, which

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:22.052
does not need worker
nodes to be pre-deployed.

00:32:26.980 --> 00:32:29.122
Sorry.

00:32:29.122 --> 00:32:30.080
All right, here you go.

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:33.140
The executor is Kubernetes here.

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:35.250
So what happens
is when you run--

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:36.500
let me connect to the new one.

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:06.250
All right, so let's look
at the configuration

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:07.700
for the new cluster.

00:33:15.430 --> 00:33:17.570
If you notice, it's using
the Kubernetes executor

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:19.180
at this point.

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:20.920
And of course the
MySQL string is

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:23.260
going to be different from
the other two clusters.

00:33:37.930 --> 00:33:39.260
I just fired.

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:42.920
You notice that it's
generating pods on the fly.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:47.220
So all the tasks in your
DAG will end up as pods.

00:33:47.220 --> 00:33:49.970
It's similar to
the Spark Operator.

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:51.620
The good part about
this is you don't

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:56.430
need to have dedicated worker
pods running all the time,

00:33:56.430 --> 00:34:00.390
and there are some use
cases where it's useful.

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:02.730
So that's the
Kubernetes executor.

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:05.840
So if you notice,
the Operator makes

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:09.260
it easy to deploy Airflow with
different configurations-- one

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:13.100
with CeleryExecutor, one
with Kubernetes executor.

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:15.679
And a developer could also
use the local or a sequential

00:34:15.679 --> 00:34:17.480
executor as well.

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:21.110
And you're able to do all
this with just two custom

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:27.080
resources against this
many StatefulSets, secrets,

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:29.580
and all these things.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:32.170
And it's not just the deployment
that the Airflow cluster

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:32.820
takes care of.

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:37.250
It also takes care of
managing data operations,

00:34:37.250 --> 00:34:40.239
like if you want to scale out.

00:34:52.170 --> 00:34:56.239
If you want to horizontally
scale a cluster that you

00:34:56.239 --> 00:34:59.450
already deployed, you can
edit the custom resource

00:34:59.450 --> 00:35:00.720
and change the replicas.

00:35:07.420 --> 00:35:08.980
So I'm going to
bump it up to four.

00:35:14.094 --> 00:35:15.760
All right, you'll see
that it's creating

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:19.880
the new workers for the first
cluster that we deployed.

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:22.460
So what does underlying
is it goes and bumps up

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:25.330
the replica for the StatefulSet,
which in turn creates

00:35:25.330 --> 00:35:28.176
new pods for you.

00:35:28.176 --> 00:35:34.030
And you could also change the
executor type of your cluster

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:35.359
once it's deployed.

00:35:35.359 --> 00:35:37.400
So, for example, if you
deployed a CeleryExecutor

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:40.190
and switch it to Kubernetes,
it deletes the worker nodes

00:35:40.190 --> 00:35:41.270
for you.

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:44.890
So it gives you that
dynamic ability.

00:35:44.890 --> 00:35:48.400
And it's not just
scaling and deployment,

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:50.810
it also makes sure your
clusters are resilient.

00:35:50.810 --> 00:35:52.330
What I mean by
that is, Kubernetes

00:35:52.330 --> 00:35:56.500
does a good job of handling
pod failures are node failures.

00:35:56.500 --> 00:36:00.590
But if someone accidentally
deletes your StatefulSet,

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:03.717
the executor monitors that
and it recreates it for you.

00:36:03.717 --> 00:36:05.550
It just makes it a
little bit more resilient

00:36:05.550 --> 00:36:10.180
than just using
Kubernetes manifests.

00:36:10.180 --> 00:36:14.430
And what else?

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:16.554
All right, I think
that's about it.

00:36:16.554 --> 00:36:17.220
Thank you, guys.

00:36:22.142 --> 00:36:23.600
PALAK DALAL BHATIA:
So if there are

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:26.016
three things that you want to
take away from this session,

00:36:26.016 --> 00:36:28.470
they are as follows.

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:30.500
Number one, Kubernetes
is no longer

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:33.440
the platform for just
running stateless services.

00:36:33.440 --> 00:36:36.560
You can now run complex stateful
applications on Kubernetes

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:38.720
and manage them in
a very easy way.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:41.812
You just saw two amazing
demos on Spark and Airflow,

00:36:41.812 --> 00:36:44.270
which could be run and managed
in just a couple of minutes.

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:49.920
Secondly, with the
use of operators

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:52.980
you can perform Day
N kind of operations,

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:54.990
not just on Google
Cloud, but on any cloud.

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:58.320
You can run those operators
on your private data centers,

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:00.690
and have the portability and
the consistent experience

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:04.290
that you want across your
clouds for the business

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:06.930
critical multi-cloud strategy.

00:37:06.930 --> 00:37:08.910
You can discover
these applications

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:11.320
on the GCP Marketplace
to begin with,

00:37:11.320 --> 00:37:14.780
including the Spark
and Airflow Operators.

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:19.980
And third, Google Cloud
provides some of the best tools

00:37:19.980 --> 00:37:22.350
to run these
applications on GKE.

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:24.570
If you want to run an
open-source service,

00:37:24.570 --> 00:37:25.950
you can use Spark and Airflow.

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:28.650
But if you want managed
service with SLAs as well

00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:30.390
as production-grade
support, you can

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:32.610
use DataPlug as well
as Cloud Composer

00:37:32.610 --> 00:37:35.250
for these managed instances.

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:37.230
You can integrate with
some of the other GCP

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:41.730
services like BigQuery, Cloud
Storage, Stackdriver, GPUs,

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:45.030
and TPUs into your
Kubernetes clusters.

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:47.170
And click to deploy
these applications

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:50.760
on GKE, not just from Google but
also from third-party partners

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:54.090
that we are working
very closely with.

00:37:54.090 --> 00:37:57.470
Thank you for your time, and
we welcome any questions.

